<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maurice Bishop International Airport</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Point Salines International Airport</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-bishop">Maurice Bishop</a> International Airport, formerly Point Salines International Airport, is the main airport of Grenada, at the south-western tip of the island. Its construction under Maurice Bishop's government with Cuban workers was seen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as a possible military threat and was cited to justify the invasion of 1983. It opened in 1984 and was renamed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> in 2009.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maurice-bishop-international-airport.jpg" alt="Maurice Bishop International Airport" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Estormiz · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Bishop_International_Airport_Grenada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The airport stands on Point Salines, the south-western point of Grenada, in the parish of St. George; the capital, St. George's, lies about 8 kilometres to the north. It was designed to replace the obsolete Pearls Airport on the north side of the island and was championed from 1979 by Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-bishop">Maurice Bishop</a>. The Grenadian government said that long-range jets carrying tourists could not land on the short, geographically difficult runway at Pearls, so visitors had to change to smaller regional planes. It hoped direct flights from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> would raise tourism. A Canadian firm designed the airport, specialised contracts went to European contractors, and two private American firms took part.</p><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a> cited the project as evidence that Grenada meant to let Soviet military aircraft use it as a way point to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, pointing in part to the Cuban workers building it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The unfinished airport was the jump-off point for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> invasion of Grenada in October 1983. The event that precipitated the invasion was not the airport but a violent coup in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> was killed. The American justification was the perceived threat to American medical students at St. George's University, whose campus is a short distance from the airfield.</p><p>At dawn on 25 October 1983 more than 500 Rangers of the 1st and 2nd Battalions of the 75th Ranger Regiment made a low-altitude <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a> assault onto the airport. Despite resistance from the People's Revolutionary Army and armed Cuban construction workers, they secured their objectives quickly. From 2:05 p.m. United States Air Force C-141 transports landed and unloaded six battalions of paratroopers of the 82nd Airborne Division.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the invasion the airport was completed with 19 million dollars of American assistance, and it received its first commercial passenger plane on 28 October 1984. In 2009 the National Democratic Congress government renamed it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a>, in the presence of his mother Alimenta Bishop. It lies 12 metres above sea level and has one asphalt runway, 10/28, measuring 2,744 by 45 metres. It houses the Grenada outstation of the Eastern Caribbean Civil Aviation Authority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice Bishop International Airport” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It expanded literacy, health and cooperatives, allied with Cuba, which helped build the <b>Point Salines</b> airport, and postponed elections.</blockquote><a href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a></li></ul></section>
